{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"DEV='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; rg -n 'PixelHUD|PixelHud|\\\\.hud\\\\.text|hud\\\\.rect|type Song|interface Song|musicVolume|setMusic|type ClipDef|interface ClipDef|class Input|moveAxis|preset.*fixed' \\\"\"'$DEV/GUIDE.md\" \"$DEV/API.md\" | head -100; sed -n '\"'115,145p' PLAN.md; sed -n '435,490p' PLAN.md; sed -n '1,100p' \\\"\"'$DEV/vendor/hub/README.md\"'","aggregated_output":"A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:175:- `setCamera(config: CameraConfig, options: { syncUrl?: boolean } = {}): CameraRig`: Swap the camera preset mid-game (review tool: presets are a per-game choice). The player, physics and the rest of the world are left exactly as they are. `free` and `fixed` without an explicit position start from the view currently on screen. Keeps `?camera=`…\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:641:gets control, and `fixedConfig()` returns the config to hard-code (preset 'fixed').\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:644:- `new FreeRig(preset: 'free' | 'fixed', config: CameraConfig = {})`\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:645:- `readonly preset: 'free' | 'fixed'`\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:2287:### class Input\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:2292:- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:2332:- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:2582:### interface Song\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:2774:  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:2775:  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/API.md:3547:### interface ClipDef\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:170:    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `COINS ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:171:    if (this.won) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'YOU WIN!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:292:between steps), `moveAxis()` (`{ x, y }` from keys, stick or touch). Codes are\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:418:| `free`, `fixed` | authoring fly-cam; a frozen view | `{ preset: 'fixed', position, target, fov }` |\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:432:ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `SCORE ${score}`, { color: 'sand' });            // 5×7 font, UPPERCASE\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:433:ctx.hud.text(6, 6, 'LIVES 3', { anchor: 'top-right' });              // anchors: top-left (default), top,\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:434:ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });        //   top-right, left, center, right, bottom-*\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:435:ctx.hud.rect(0, 0, ctx.hud.width, 12, 'ink', 'bottom-left');\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:668:    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `LEVEL ${this.level + 1}`, { color: 'sand' });\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:669:    ctx.hud.text(6, 16, `TIME ${time.toFixed(1)}  STOMPS ${this.stomps}`);\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:670:    if (this.clearedAt !== null) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'LEVEL CLEAR!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:842:    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, this.escaped ? 'YOU ESCAPED!' : this.solved ? 'THE GATE IS OPEN' : `PADS ${lit}/${this.pads.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:950:    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `LOOK ${this.current + 1}/${LOOKS.length}: ${LOOKS[this.current][0].toUpperCase()}`, { color: 'sand' });\nA Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846/GUIDE.md:951:    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, '1-6 LOOKS  T CHUNKY', { anchor: 'bottom-left' });\nEverything hangs on this feeling good, so it comes first. A stick mapped straight to position would be trivial, because you would just hold still. So the Dot is an unstable weight: you apply force, and it drifts, sways and overshoots.\n\n### The model ✔ (simulated with human-like bots; these are the defaults)\n\n```js\n// src/core/dot.js: pure, no engine imports. Step in fixedUpdate at 60 Hz. p is in -1.2..1.2.\nexport class Dot {\n  x = 0; y = 0; vx = 0; vy = 0; ux = 0; uy = 0; t = 0;\n  constructor(rand) { this.ph = [rand(), rand(), rand(), rand()].map((r) => r * 6.283); }\n  step(dt, inX, inY, T, m = {}) { // T: tunables. m: per-activity modifiers.\n    this.t += dt;\n    const k = Math.min(1, dt / T.dotLag);\n    this.ux += (inX - this.ux) * k; this.uy += (inY - this.uy) * k;\n    const n = T.dotNoise * (m.noise ?? 1), inst = T.dotInstability * (m.inst ?? 1), auth = T.dotAuthority * (m.auth ?? 1);\n    const nx = n * (Math.sin(1.7 * this.t + this.ph[0]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(3.1 * this.t + this.ph[1]));\n    const ny = n * (Math.sin(1.3 * this.t + this.ph[2]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(2.7 * this.t + this.ph[3]));\n    this.vx += (inst * this.x + auth * this.ux - T.dotDamping * this.vx + nx + (m.bx ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.vy += (inst * this.y + auth * this.uy - T.dotDamping * this.vy + ny + (m.by ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.x += this.vx * dt; this.y += this.vy * dt;\n    const d = Math.hypot(this.x, this.y);\n    if (d > 1.2) { const s = 1.2 / d; this.x *= s; this.y *= s; this.vx *= 0.3; this.vy *= 0.3; }\n  }\n}\n// Defaults: dotInstability 0.6, dotAuthority 3.0, dotDamping 3.4, dotNoise 0.8, dotLag 0.08\n```\n\nTop speed is about 0.9 units/s, so a lean feels like shifting a heavy marble. Impulses (a sneeze, a gust) add directly to `vx`/`vy`.\n\n### Lean input\n\nAll devices produce one vector `u` in the unit disc, read per lane through a **driver** (section 16).\n\n**One UI for every device: the pixel HUD.** No DOM menus; same screens for pad, keyboard and mouse. Build a small kit in `src/ui/`.\n\n### Layout rules\n\n- **Use `aspect: 'fill'` ✔.** It removes letterboxing at every window size (`adaptive` gives thick black borders at 1280×720). Art size then varies: 480×270 at 1080p and 4K, 427×240 at 720p, 640×360 in a small frame, 400×300 at 800×600. **Lay out from `hud.width` and `hud.height` with anchors**, never fixed coordinates. Keep a 12 art px safe margin.\n- **Pointer to art pixels ✔:** `ax = (p.x + 1) / 2 * hud.width; ay = (1 - p.y) / 2 * hud.height`. The HUD canvas ignores pointer events, and its rect equals the game canvas's.\n- **Text:** the 5×7 font at scale 1 is 20×28 px at 1080p, the minimum. Scale 2 for menu rows and headings, 3-4 for the title and big numbers.\n- **No dense screens:** at most 8 menu rows; at most 3 lines of dialogue; one idea per screen; one activity per records page. Page rather than scroll, wherever you can.\n\n### Mixed-case text ✔\n\nThe engine font is capitals only, which is wrong for this voice. Draw lowercase through `hud.sprite`, everything else through `hud.text`, on a 6 px grid. This table and function rendered correctly; use them:\n\n```js\n// src/ui/font.js. Rows top to bottom, 5 bits, bit 4 = left. Rows 7-8 are descenders.\nconst H = {\na:'00 00 0e 01 0f 11 0f 00 00', b:'10 10 1e 11 11 11 1e 00 00', c:'00 00 0e 11 10 11 0e 00 00', d:'01 01 0f 11 11 11 0f 00 00',\ne:'00 00 0e 11 1f 10 0e 00 00', f:'06 09 08 1c 08 08 08 00 00', g:'00 00 0f 11 11 11 0f 01 0e', h:'10 10 1e 11 11 11 11 00 00',\ni:'04 00 0c 04 04 04 0e 00 00', j:'02 00 06 02 02 02 02 12 0c', k:'10 10 12 14 18 14 12 00 00', l:'0c 04 04 04 04 04 0e 00 00',\nm:'00 00 1a 15 15 15 15 00 00', n:'00 00 1e 11 11 11 11 00 00', o:'00 00 0e 11 11 11 0e 00 00', p:'00 00 1e 11 11 11 1e 10 10',\nq:'00 00 0f 11 11 11 0f 01 01', r:'00 00 16 19 10 10 10 00 00', s:'00 00 0f 10 0e 01 1e 00 00', t:'08 08 1c 08 08 09 06 00 00',\nu:'00 00 11 11 11 13 0d 00 00', v:'00 00 11 11 11 0a 04 00 00', w:'00 00 11 11 15 15 0a 00 00', x:'00 00 11 0a 04 0a 11 00 00',\ny:'00 00 11 11 11 11 0f 01 0e', z:'00 00 1f 02 04 08 1f 00 00' };\nexport const GLYPHS = Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(H).map(([k, v]) => [k, v.split(' ').map((h) => parseInt(h, 16).toString(2).padStart(5, '0').replace(/0/g, '.').replace(/1/g, 'o'))]));\nexport function say(hud, x, y, text, { color = 'white', shadow = 'ink', scale = 1 } = {}) {\n  for (const ch of text) {\n    const g = GLYPHS[ch];\n    if (g) hud.sprite(x, y, g, { o: color }, { scale, shadow });\n    else if (ch !== ' ') hud.text(x, y, ch, { color, shadow, scale });\n    x += 6 * scale;\n  }\n  return x;\n}\n```\n\nAdd a measuring and word-wrapping helper. Replace any character the font lacks with `?` (hub names can hold anything); a test checks all game text has glyphs.\n\n### Input map\n\nRemap once at boot ✔: `ctx.input.gamepadButtons = { 0:'PadCross', 1:'PadCircle', 2:'PadSquare', 3:'PadTriangle', 4:'PadL1', 5:'PadR1', 6:'PadL2', 7:'PadR2', 8:'PadCreate', 9:'PadOptions', 10:'PadL3', 11:'PadR3', 12:'PadUp', 13:'PadDown', 14:'PadLeft', 15:'PadRight' }`. Pad buttons then appear in `isDown`, `wasPressed`, `state().held` and recordings. Keep one action table in `src/core/actions.js`.\n\n| Action | DualSense (Xbox glyph) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lean | Left stick | WASD / arrows; Shift = half | Pointer offset from dial |\n| Confirm | ✕ (A) | Enter, Space, E | Left click on the item |\n| Back | ○ (B) | Esc, Backspace | Right click |\n| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc, P | Pause icon, top right, shown while the mouse moves |\n| Breath, hold | R2 (RT) or ✕ (A) | Space | Hold left button |\n| Left / right foot | L1 or L2 / R1 or R2 (LB or LT / RB or RT) | F or ← / J or → | Left / right button |\n| Tabs | L1 / R1 (LB / RB) | Q / E | Wheel, or click the tab |\n| Hub: jump, wave, sit | ✕, △, □ (A, Y, X) | Space, V, B | Click to walk |\n\n- **Hero wrapper ✔:** call `readMoveInput(ctx, hero, input)`, then OR in `PadCross` (`consumePress` → `input.jump`, `isDown` → `input.jumpHeld`) and the same for wave and sit. In the hub ✕ interacts when a prompt is showing, otherwise jumps.\n- **Menus** read the d-pad, stick (with repeat), arrows and WASD. Hover moves focus; left click confirms; a wheel scrolls lists; sliders drag.\n- **Keep the mouse native:** the Dot follows the mouse as soon as the mouse moves, and goes back to stick or keys as soon as those are used.\n# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\n\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\n\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\n\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\n\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\n\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\n\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\n\n---\n\n## Modes\n\nA game declares one mode.\n\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\n|---|---|---|\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\n\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\n  world the host sends them.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\n\n---\n\n## A round\n\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\n   (*The card*).\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\n4. The game reports how it goes.\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\n   over the game, then removes it.\n\n### Tags\n\nIn the game's `<head>`:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"MIN-MAX\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"MODE\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"GOAL\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"CONTROLS\">\n```\n\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\n  is in that range.\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\n\nOptional:\n\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\n\n### The client\n\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\n\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_41","type":"reasoning","text":"**Pinpointing the exact engine**"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"DEV='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; sed -n '118,143p' PLAN.md; sed -n '2287,2337p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'2490,2635p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'2740,2805p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'3520,3598p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"DEV='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; sed -n '118,143p' PLAN.md; sed -n '2287,2337p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'2490,2635p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'2740,2805p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'3520,3598p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"'","aggregated_output":"\n```js\n// src/core/dot.js: pure, no engine imports. Step in fixedUpdate at 60 Hz. p is in -1.2..1.2.\nexport class Dot {\n  x = 0; y = 0; vx = 0; vy = 0; ux = 0; uy = 0; t = 0;\n  constructor(rand) { this.ph = [rand(), rand(), rand(), rand()].map((r) => r * 6.283); }\n  step(dt, inX, inY, T, m = {}) { // T: tunables. m: per-activity modifiers.\n    this.t += dt;\n    const k = Math.min(1, dt / T.dotLag);\n    this.ux += (inX - this.ux) * k; this.uy += (inY - this.uy) * k;\n    const n = T.dotNoise * (m.noise ?? 1), inst = T.dotInstability * (m.inst ?? 1), auth = T.dotAuthority * (m.auth ?? 1);\n    const nx = n * (Math.sin(1.7 * this.t + this.ph[0]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(3.1 * this.t + this.ph[1]));\n    const ny = n * (Math.sin(1.3 * this.t + this.ph[2]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(2.7 * this.t + this.ph[3]));\n    this.vx += (inst * this.x + auth * this.ux - T.dotDamping * this.vx + nx + (m.bx ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.vy += (inst * this.y + auth * this.uy - T.dotDamping * this.vy + ny + (m.by ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.x += this.vx * dt; this.y += this.vy * dt;\n    const d = Math.hypot(this.x, this.y);\n    if (d > 1.2) { const s = 1.2 / d; this.x *= s; this.y *= s; this.vx *= 0.3; this.vy *= 0.3; }\n  }\n}\n// Defaults: dotInstability 0.6, dotAuthority 3.0, dotDamping 3.4, dotNoise 0.8, dotLag 0.08\n```\n\nTop speed is about 0.9 units/s, so a lean feels like shifting a heavy marble. Impulses (a sneeze, a gust) add directly to `vx`/`vy`.\n\n### Lean input\n### class Input\n\nKeyboard + pointer + gamepad input.\n- Keys: held state, per-frame presses (`wasPressed`) and queued presses for fixed-step\n  code (`consumePress`). Queued presses expire after `pressWindow` s of game time.\n- Gamepads (standard mapping): the left stick feeds `moveAxis()` (radial deadzone), the\n  right stick feeds the pointer delta (camera look), buttons press key codes\n  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n- Pointer: accumulated movement per frame (while a button is held or the pointer is\n  locked), wheel delta per frame, optional pointer lock (first-person). Mouse buttons on\n  the canvas are keys too (`MOUSE_BUTTONS`: `MouseLeft`, `MouseRight`, `MouseMiddle`), and a\n  finger is `Touch` (`TOUCH_KEY`), held and pressed like any other, so a click shorter than\n  a frame is not lost.\n- Commands (`send`): data from outside the canvas, delivered to the game…\n\n- `new Input(target: Window | null = window)`\n- `readonly mouseDelta: { x: number; y: number; }`: Pointer movement this frame, in CSS pixels (drag or pointer lock).\n- `readonly pointer: { x: number; y: number; over: boolean; touch: boolean; }`: Where the pointer last was over the canvas, in normalised device coordinates (−1..1, y up: `camera.rayAt(pointer.x, pointer.y, …)` turns it into a ray), and whether it is over it now. Under pointer lock it is the screen's centre (the crosshair).\n- `wheel: number`: Wheel ticks this frame (+1 = scroll down / zoom out).\n- `wantsPointerLock: boolean`: When true, clicking the canvas requests pointer lock (first-person / free camera).\n- `readonly analog: { x: number; y: number; }`: Touch joystick, -1..1 per axis; overrides keys when active.\n- `readonly padAxis: { x: number; y: number; }`: Gamepad left stick after the deadzone, -1..1 (y = forward).\n- `gamepadConnected: boolean`: True while any gamepad is connected.\n- `readonly preventKeys: Set<string>`: Extra key codes whose browser default is blocked (arrows and Space always are): a game's own F1 / F2 / Tab panels, so the browser's help page or focus change doesn't fire.\n- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n- `gamepadDeadzone: number`: Stick deadzone (radial, 0..1).\n- `gamepadLookSpeed: number`: Right stick look speed: pointer pixels per second at full tilt.\n- `now: number`: Game time (s) of the current frame; set by the engine through `beginFrame`.\n- `get playing: boolean`: True while a replay drives input (`engine.replay()`): keyboard, pointer and wheel events, `setKey` / `setPointer` / `setButtons` / `addPointer` (touch buttons, scripts) and `send` are ignored, and `playBefore` / `playAfter` set everything.\n- `pressWindow: number`: Queued presses older than this many seconds of game time are dropped.\n- `attachPointer(el: HTMLElement): void`: Listen for pointer input on the game canvas. Only pointers that went down on the canvas drive the camera (so a touch joystick doesn't orbit it). Two canvas touches pinch to zoom (emitted as wheel ticks).\n- `dispose(): void`: Remove every listener this Input added (window and canvas).\n- `release(): void`: Let go of everything held (keys, gamepad buttons, sticks, pointer buttons); queued presses stay.\n- `reset(): void`: Forget held keys, presses, sticks and pointer movement (e.g. when a level loads).\n- `beginFrame(now: number, dt: number, pads?: readonly (GamepadLike | null)[]): void`: Engine: begin a frame at game time `now` (seconds). Polls gamepads and drops expired queued presses. `pads` defaults to `navigator.getGamepads()`; tests can pass fakes.\n- `get pointerLocked: boolean`\n- `isDown(...codes: string[]): boolean`\n- `anyDown(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `isDown` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array (fixed-step code).\n- `wasPressed(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press (valid for the current render frame).\n- `consumePress(...codes: string[]): boolean`: True once per physical key press, kept until consumed or until it is older than `pressWindow` (150 ms of game time). Use from fixed-step code (which may run zero or several times per frame) so no press is dropped, while a stale press (made during a pause, a l…\n- `consumeAny(codes: readonly string[]): boolean`: `consumePress` for a list of codes, without building a rest-argument array.\n- `state(): InputState`: Held keys, queued presses and the pointer as data (`engine.snapshot()`), between frames.\n- `setState(s: InputState, now: number): void`: Back to `state()` at game time `now` (a restore): held keys are those the snapshot held, live ones let go.\n- `get queuedPresses: string[]`: Presses waiting to be consumed (tooling / tests).\n- `clearQueued(...codes: string[]): void`: Drop queued presses (e.g. when a menu or mode swallows input).\n- `moveAxis(): { x: number; y: number }`: -1..1 on each axis (x = right, y = forward): the touch joystick or a gamepad's left stick (whichever is tilted further), else WASD / arrow keys / d-pad.\n- `endFrame(): void`: Call at the end of each rendered frame.\n- `setKey(code: string, down: boolean): void`: Test hook: simulate a key being held (true) or released (false).\n- `setPointer(x: number, y: number, down?: boolean): void`: Test hook: put the pointer at (x, y) in normalised device coordinates, pressed or not.\n- `setButtons(bits: number): void`: Test hook: hold mouse buttons (`PointerEvent.buttons` bits: 1 left, 2 right, 4 middle) as the canvas would.\n- `get mouseButtons: number`: Buttons held, as `PointerEvent.buttons` bits (1 left, 2 right, 4 middle; a finger is left): the same keys as `isDown('MouseLeft')`.\n- `playMusic(song: Song | null, name = 'song'): void`: Start a song (looping by default), replacing the current one. `null` stops music.\n- `stopMusic(): void`\n- `persist: boolean`: Volume changes are written to storage. The engine turns this off while a replay plays: the recording sets the volumes it set, nothing is written, and the player's come back after.\n- `setVolume(channel: VolumeChannel, value: number): void`\n- `setMuted(muted: boolean): void`\n- `toggleMute(): boolean`\n- `resetVolumes(): void`: The default volumes, sound on (the settings panel's Reset).\n- `update(): void`: Engine: once per rendered frame. Schedules the next notes of the song.\n- `dispose(): void`: Stop everything and close the AudioContext.\n\n### interface AudioSettings\n\n- `master: number`\n- `sfx: number`\n- `music: number`\n- `muted: boolean`\n\n### type Instrument\n\n```ts\ntype Instrument = Omit<SoundDef, 'freq' | 'freqEnd' | 'sustain' | 'delay'>\n```\n\nA track's instrument: a SoundDef without pitch or length (each note sets those).\n\n### interface Listener\n\n- `position: V3Like`\n- `right: V3Like`: The listener's right, in world space (unit): the camera's right for a third-person game.\n\n### muffleCutoff\n\n```ts\nconst muffleCutoff: (muffle: number) => number\n```\n\nLow-pass cutoff (Hz) for a muffle of 0..1: wide open to a dull thud.\n\n### noteFrequency()\n\n```ts\nfunction noteFrequency(note: string): number | null\n```\n\n'A4' → 440, 'C#5', 'Eb3'… Returns null for anything that is not a note.\n\n### parseSong()\n\n```ts\nfunction parseSong(song: Song): ParsedSong\n```\n\nFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n\n### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n\n```ts\nconst PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song\n```\n\nThe playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps.\n\n### interface PlayOptions\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n\n### renderSound()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n```\n\nRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n\n### SFX\n\n```ts\nconst SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n```\n\nBuilt-in retro sound effects, as data. Tweak numbers here (or register your own with\n`ctx.audio.register(name, def)`) and every `ctx.audio.play(name)` picks them up.\nSee docs/engine/audio.md for the SoundDef fields.\n\n### type SfxName\n\n```ts\ntype SfxName = keyof typeof SFX\n```\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n### interface SoundDef\n\n- `wave: Wave`\n- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n- `sustain?: number`\n- `decay?: number`\n- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n- `arpRate?: number`\n- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n\n### interface Spatial\n\n- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n- `pan: number`: -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `distance: number`\n\n### spatialize()\n\n```ts\nfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n```\n\nGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n\n### interface SpatialOptions\n\n- `ref?: number`: Within this distance (m) the sound is at full volume (default 1).\n- `max?: number`: Silent from this distance on (default 30); it fades out over the last fifth.\n- `rolloff?: number`: How fast it falls off past `ref` (default 1: inverse distance, the way sound really does).\n\n### interface Voice\n\n- `readonly presets: Map<string, ParticlePreset>`: Preset registry: the built-ins plus anything registered.\n- `readonly pixelWorld: UniformNode<\"float\", number>`: World units per art pixel at the camera focus (updated every frame).\n- `register(name: string, preset: ParticlePreset): void`: Add or replace a named preset. Replacing frees the old preset's emitter.\n- `burst(preset: string | ParticlePreset, at: Vector3 | readonly [number, number, number], options: BurstOptions = {}): number`: Spawn a burst of `preset` (a name or a ParticlePreset) at `at`. Returns the number of particles spawned (fewer when the pool is full, 0 for an unknown preset). Inline preset objects share one emitter per distinct content, so `burst({ ...literal }, at)` every…\n- `get alive: number`: Particles alive right now, over all emitters.\n- `get emitterCount: number`: Emitters (= draw calls at most) currently allocated.\n- `update(dt: number): void`: Simulate and upload. Called by the engine every frame (frozen while paused).\n- `reseed(rng: Rng): void`: Take bursts' randomness from `rng` (the engine passes each level's `ctx.random` fork).\n- `clear(): void`: Kill every particle and free the emitters (GPU buffers, materials).\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### PARTICLES\n\n```ts\nconst PARTICLES: { dust: { count: [number, number]; life: [number, number]; speed: [number, number]; spread: number; flatten: number; gravity: number; drag: number; size: [number, number]; colors: (\"white\" | \"mist\" | \"slate\")[]; radius: number; }; skid: { ...; }; sparkle: { ...; }; smoke: { ...; }; impact: { ...; }; }\n```\n\nBuilt-in particle effects, as data (see ParticlePreset). Register more with\n`ctx.particles.register(name, preset)`. Sizes are art pixels; colors are PALETTE names.\n\n## 10. HUD\n\n### class Hud\n\nPixel HUD: crisp text and icons drawn in art pixels on top of the game, never through\nthe post filters. It is a 2D canvas at the internal resolution (480×270 / 320×180),\nlaid over the game canvas with the same integer scale and letterbox offset\n(`framing.ts`), so one HUD pixel is exactly one art pixel at every resolution.\n\nRetained: what you add stays on screen until `clear()`. Redraw everything each frame\n(clear + add) or only when something changes; the canvas repaints only when the content,\nresolution or framing changed. Available as `ctx.hud`.\n\n  ctx.hud.clear();\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, `COINS ${n}/12`, { color: 'sand' });\n  ctx.hud.text(4, 4, 'PAUSED', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2 });\n\n- `new Hud(container: HTMLElement | null)`\n- `canvas: HTMLCanvasElement | null`: The overlay canvas (created on the first draw; null when nothing was ever drawn).\n- `visible: boolean`\n- `get width: number`: HUD width/height in art pixels (the internal resolution).\n- `get height: number`\n- `text(x: number, y: number, text: string, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: Text in the 5×7 bitmap font (uppercase; digits and common punctuation).\n- `rect(x: number, y: number, w: number, h: number, color: HudColor, anchor: HudAnchor = 'top-left'): void`: A filled rectangle (panels, bars).\n- `sprite(x: number, y: number, rows: readonly string[], colors: Record<string, HudColor>, options: HudOptions = {}): void`: A tiny pixel-art icon as data: one string per row, one character per pixel, mapped through `colors` ('.' or ' ' = transparent).\n- `clear(): void`: Remove everything.\n- `measure(text: string, scale = 1): { width: number; height: number }`: Size of `text` in art pixels at `scale`.\n- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n- `dispose(): void`\n\n### type HudAnchor\n\n```ts\ntype HudAnchor = 'top-left' | 'top' | 'top-right' | 'left' | 'center' | 'right' | 'bottom-left' | 'bottom' | 'bottom-right'\n```\n\nWhere an element is attached on screen; it is also aligned that way (right-anchored text grows left).\n\n### type HudColor\n\n```ts\ntype HudColor = PaletteColor | number\n```\n\n### interface HudOptions\n\n### interface VariantGroup\n\nOne def standing for its variants (the defs that share its `variantName`), and the others.\n\n- `readonly def: T`\n- `readonly variants: readonly T[]`\n\n### variantName()\n\n```ts\nfunction variantName(id: string): string\n```\n\nThe name a model's variants share: its id without its last part that is one letter or a\nnumber, at the end or before the rest of the name (`kenney-nature/rock-large-a` … `-f`,\n`crate-1` … `-3`, `tree-pine-tall-a-detailed` … `-d-detailed` → `kenney-nature/rock-large`,\n`crate`, `tree-pine-tall-detailed`). An id without one is its own. For models: a sheet's\nlettered tiles (`forest-a` … `-i`) are pieces of a set, not variants.\n\n## 12. Animation toolkit\n\n### analyzeClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction analyzeClip(model: Object3D, rig: RigSpec, def: ClipDef, clip: AnimationClip): ClipReport\n```\n\n### interface ClipDef\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`: Length in frames at 30 fps.\n- `loop?: boolean`\n- `keys: readonly Key[]`\n- `layers?: readonly Layer[]`\n- `feet?: readonly FeetKey[]`: Feet track: foot goals interpolated between its own keys and solved with IK every frame, overriding the keyed leg rotations. Use it whenever feet touch the floor through a transition (kneel → stand, sit → lie) so they roll instead of sinking.\n- `speed?: number`: Ground speed (m/s) the clip is authored for at playback rate 1. Locomotion clips set this; metrics then measure foot sliding against it and the runtime can scale playback.\n- `stance?: number`: Gait cycles (gaitClip): the share of the cycle each foot is planted. The right heel strikes at phase 0, the left at 0.5, so the right foot is mid-stance at `stance / 2`.\n- `reach?: number`: Gaits: how far ahead of the hips (m) the middle of the stance is (feet touch down `reach` + half a stance's ground ahead).\n- `grounded?: boolean`: Grounded clip: soles should rest on y = 0 (metrics flag floating / penetration).\n- `fast?: boolean`: Intentionally snappy (flips, punches, launches): skip the angular-speed warning.\n- `notes?: string`\n\n### interface ClipReport\n\nObjective checks for a clip, computed by actually posing the rig (no renderer). These\nare the numbers an agent can't eyeball: foot sliding, ground contact, loop seams,\nvelocity spikes, joint limits. Thresholds are deliberately forgiving; `problems` are\nreal defects, `warnings` are worth a look.\n\n- `name: string`\n- `frames: number`\n- `loop: boolean`\n- `speed: number | null`\n- `minSoleY: number`: Lowest sole point over the clip (m) and the frame it happens. Negative = through the floor.\n- `minSoleFrame: number`\n- `minBodyY: number`: Lowest point of any other body part (knees, hands, head...), where and when.\n- `minBodyMesh: string`\n- `minBodyFrame: number`\n- `floatingFrames: number`: Frames (of grounded clips) where neither sole is within 5 cm of the floor.\n- `footSlide: number`: Mean horizontal slip of the planted foot, m/s (0 = perfectly planted).\n- `loopSeam: number`: Largest joint-angle jump between the last and first frame of a loop, degrees.\n- `maxAngularSpeed: { joint: string; degPerSec: number; frame: number }`: Fastest joint rotation, degrees per second, and where.\n- `limitViolations: { joint: string; axis: 'x' | 'y' | 'z'; frame: number; value: number; range: [number, number] }[]`\n- `pelvisY: [number, number]`: Pelvis height range (m) — how much the body bobs.\n- `problems: string[]`\n- `warnings: string[]`\n\n### compileClip()\n\n```ts\nfunction compileClip(def: ClipDef, rig: RigSpec, rest: RestPose): AnimationClip\n```\n\nBake a ClipDef into a three.js AnimationClip (quaternion + position + scale tracks,\nsampled at 60 Hz so easing and per-axis Euler interpolation — including 360° flips —\nsurvive exactly).\n\n### compileClips()\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_43","type":"reasoning","text":"**Inspecting docs**"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"DEV='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; sed -n '90,180p' \\\"\"'$DEV/GUIDE.md\"; sed -n '\"'2540,2635p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; rg -n '\"'\"'^### (class Audio|interface Sound|interface Song|interface Note|interface Game|interface EngineOptions|interface GameContext|interface CameraConfig|function loadModel|const HERO|class Engine)'\"' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'95,240p' \\\"\"'$DEV/vendor/hub/README.md\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"DEV='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; sed -n '90,180p' \\\"\"'$DEV/GUIDE.md\"; sed -n '\"'2540,2635p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; rg -n '\"'\"'^### (class Audio|interface Sound|interface Song|interface Note|interface Game|interface EngineOptions|interface GameContext|interface CameraConfig|function loadModel|const HERO|class Engine)'\"' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'95,240p' \\\"\"'$DEV/vendor/hub/README.md\"'","aggregated_output":"// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\nimport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n\nconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n\n/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\nfunction block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\n  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\n  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\n  return mesh;\n}\n\nclass CoinGarden {\n  name = 'Coin Garden';\n  assets = [HERO_MODEL, 'assets/coin.glb']; // start downloading before setup runs\n  coins = [];\n  collected = 0;\n  won = false;\n  target = new Vector3();\n  ground = new Vector3();\n\n  async setup(ctx) {\n    const { scene, physics, palette } = ctx;\n    // Level: static geometry merged into one draw call per material; one collider per block.\n    scene.add(\n      ...mergeStaticMeshes([\n        block(ctx, [0, -0.5, 0], [24, 1, 24], palette.green),\n        block(ctx, [4, 0.5, -2], [3, 1, 3], palette.slate),\n        block(ctx, [7, 1.25, -2], [3, 2.5, 3], palette.teal),\n        block(ctx, [-5, 0.75, 4], [2, 1.5, 2], palette.orange),\n      ]),\n    );\n    // Coins: a model, spinning; a trigger volume collects it when the hero walks in.\n    const coin = await ctx.loadModel('assets/coin.glb', { castShadow: false });\n    for (const [x, y, z] of [[4, 1.4, -2], [7, 2.9, -2], [-5, 1.9, 4], [-2, 0.4, -5]]) {\n      const mesh = coin.scene.clone(true);\n      mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n      setLookLayer(mesh, 'actors'); // characters & objects can get their own look\n      scene.add(mesh);\n      const item = { mesh, taken: false };\n      physics.trigger({ cylinder: { halfHeight: 0.6, radius: 0.5 } }, [x, y, z], { tag: 'character', once: true, onEnter: () => this.collect(ctx, item) });\n      this.coins.push(item);\n    }\n    // The hero: the bundled model and clips on a Mario-64-style controller.\n    const hero = await ctx.loadModel(HERO_MODEL, { castShadow: false });\n    this.model = hero.scene;\n    setLookLayer(this.model, 'actors');\n    this.shadow = new ContactShadow(0.42);\n    scene.add(this.model, this.shadow);\n    this.hero = new PlatformerCharacter(physics, { position: [0, 0, 4], lockDepth: ctx.camera.lockDepth });\n    this.hero.attachModel(this.model, compileClips(HERO_CLIPS, HERO_RIG, this.model), HERO_RIG);\n  }\n\n  collect(ctx, item) {\n    item.taken = true;\n    item.mesh.visible = false;\n    this.collected++;\n    ctx.audio.play('coin');\n    ctx.particles.burst('sparkle', item.mesh.position);\n  }\n\n  fixedUpdate(ctx, dt) {\n    this.input = readMoveInput(ctx, this.hero, this.input); // filled in place after the first step\n    this.hero.fixedUpdate(dt, this.input); // movement and forces: fixed 60 Hz step\n  }\n\n  update(ctx, dt) {\n    this.hero.updateVisual(this.model, dt, ctx.physics.alpha); // pose and interpolate\n    const feet = this.model.position;\n    const ground = ctx.physics.groundBelow(this.ground.set(feet.x, feet.y + 0.1, feet.z), 20, this.hero.body);\n    if (ground) this.shadow.place(feet.x, ground.y, feet.z, feet.y - ground.y);\n    for (const c of this.coins) if (!c.taken) c.mesh.rotation.y += dt * 3;\n    if (!this.won && this.collected === this.coins.length) {\n      this.won = true;\n      this.hero.celebrate();\n      ctx.audio.play('fanfare');\n    }\n    ctx.hud.clear();\n    ctx.hud.text(6, 6, `COINS ${this.collected}/${this.coins.length}`, { color: 'sand' });\n    if (this.won) ctx.hud.text(0, 0, 'YOU WIN!', { anchor: 'center', scale: 2, shadow: 'navy' });\n  }\n\n  cameraTarget() {\n    const p = this.model?.position ?? this.target.set(0, 0, 4);\n    return this.target.set(p.x, p.y + 0.9, p.z);\n  }\n\n  onCameraChange(ctx) {\n    this.hero.setLockDepth(ctx.camera.lockDepth); // side view: stay on the lane\n```\n\nFlatten a song into timed notes. Throws on malformed patterns, so tests catch typos.\n\n### PLAYGROUND_SONG\n\n```ts\nconst PLAYGROUND_SONG: Song\n```\n\nThe playground's short looping track: C major, bouncy, 2 × 16 steps.\n\n### interface PlayOptions\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 multiplier on the sound's own volume.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones (playback rate), e.g. a rising combo.\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n\n### renderSound()\n\n```ts\nfunction renderSound(def: SoundDef, sampleRate = 44100, seed = 1): Float32Array<ArrayBuffer>\n```\n\nRender a sound to mono samples in -1..1.\n\n### SFX\n\n```ts\nconst SFX: { jump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; attack: number; sustain: number; decay: number; volume: number; }; doubleJump: { wave: \"square\"; duty: number; freq: number; freqEnd: number; ... 5 more ...; volume: number; }; ... 8 more ...; fanfare: { ...; }; }\n```\n\nBuilt-in retro sound effects, as data. Tweak numbers here (or register your own with\n`ctx.audio.register(name, def)`) and every `ctx.audio.play(name)` picks them up.\nSee docs/engine/audio.md for the SoundDef fields.\n\n### type SfxName\n\n```ts\ntype SfxName = keyof typeof SFX\n```\n\n### interface Song\n\nA chiptune song as data. Each pattern holds one string per track: space-separated steps,\nwhere a step is a note ('C4', 'F#5', 'Bb2'), '.' (rest) or '-' (hold the previous note).\nEvery track of a pattern must have the same number of steps. For `noise` tracks the\nnote's pitch is the noise rate: C6 is a snare-ish hiss, C8 a hi-hat tick.\n\n- `bpm: number`\n- `stepsPerBeat?: number`: Steps per beat (4 = sixteenth notes). Default 4.\n- `tracks: Record<string, Instrument>`\n- `patterns: Record<string, Record<string, string>>`\n- `order: readonly string[]`: Pattern names in play order.\n- `loop?: boolean`: Loop forever (default true).\n\n### interface SoundDef\n\n- `wave: Wave`\n- `freq: number`: Start pitch in Hz. For `noise` it is the sample-and-hold rate (higher = hissier).\n- `freqEnd?: number`: End pitch for a sweep (exponential glide from `freq`).\n- `sweepTime?: number`: Seconds the sweep takes (default: the whole sound).\n- `duty?: number`: Square duty cycle: 0.125, 0.25 or 0.5 are the classic NES widths. Default 0.5.\n- `attack?: number`: Envelope (seconds): ramp up, hold at full volume, then fade out.\n- `sustain?: number`\n- `decay?: number`\n- `volume?: number`: Peak level 0..1 (default 0.5).\n- `arp?: readonly number[]`: Arpeggio: semitone offsets stepped every `arpRate` seconds (the coin \"ba-ding\").\n- `arpRate?: number`\n- `vibrato?: { depth: number; rate: number }`: Vibrato depth in semitones and rate in Hz.\n- `crush?: number`: Quantize to this many levels (4-bit style crunch). 0 = off.\n- `delay?: number`: Seconds of silence before the sound starts (for layers).\n- `layers?: readonly SoundDef[]`: Extra sounds mixed in (e.g. a noise burst under a thump).\n\n### interface Spatial\n\n- `gain: number`: 0..1 volume.\n- `pan: number`: -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `distance: number`\n\n### spatialize()\n\n```ts\nfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n```\n\nGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n\n### interface SpatialOptions\n\n- `ref?: number`: Within this distance (m) the sound is at full volume (default 1).\n- `max?: number`: Silent from this distance on (default 30); it fades out over the last fifth.\n- `rolloff?: number`: How fast it falls off past `ref` (default 1: inverse distance, the way sound really does).\n\n### interface Voice\n\n132:### class Engine\n203:### interface EngineOptions\n266:### interface Game\n288:### interface GameContext\n550:### interface CameraConfig\n2278:### interface GamepadLike\n2462:### class AudioManager\n2582:### interface Song\n2596:### interface SoundDef\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\n\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\nedited copy is not replaced.\n\n### Joining\n\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\n\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\n\n### The card\n\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\n\n```js\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\n```\n\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\nit deserves.\n\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\n\n- the game's name;\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\n- how to win the round;\n- the controls this player will use;\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\n\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\non the card:\n\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\n\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\n\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\n\n### The start\n\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\n`start`.\n\n### Reporting\n\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\n  by the host only:\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\n    placed last.\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\n\n### Time-up and the end\n\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\n  `finish()` by then is void.\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\n  game, then removes it.\n\n| `end` reason | |\n|---|---|\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\n\n### Leaving\n\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\n  that player leaving.\n\n### Scoring\n\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\n  progress.\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\n  result instead of medals.\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
