{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"DEV='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; mkdir -p \\\"\"'$DEV/src/core\" \"$DEV/src/data\" \"$DEV/src/ui\" \"$DEV/tools\" \"$DEV/tests\"; sed -n '\"'110,124p' \\\"\"'$DEV/vendor/hub/README.md\"; sed -n '\"'372,398p' \\\"\"'$DEV/vendor/hub/README.md\"; sed -n '\"'2620,2665p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'65,100p' \\\"\"'$DEV/GUIDE.md\"; rg -n '\"'GAMEPAD_BUTTONS|gamepadButtons|resolution:|aspect:' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\" | head -25'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_49","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"DEV='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; mkdir -p \\\"\"'$DEV/src/core\" \"$DEV/src/data\" \"$DEV/src/ui\" \"$DEV/tools\" \"$DEV/tests\"; sed -n '\"'110,124p' \\\"\"'$DEV/vendor/hub/README.md\"; sed -n '\"'372,398p' \\\"\"'$DEV/vendor/hub/README.md\"; sed -n '\"'2620,2665p' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\"; sed -n '\"'65,100p' \\\"\"'$DEV/GUIDE.md\"; rg -n '\"'GAMEPAD_BUTTONS|gamepadButtons|resolution:|aspect:' \\\"\"'$DEV/API.md\" | head -25'","aggregated_output":"  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\n\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\n\n| Field | |\n|---|---|\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\n### API\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\n\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\nread them.\n\n| Event | Arguments |\n|---|---|\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\n| `message` | `data, from` |\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\n### spatialize()\n\n```ts\nfunction spatialize(listener: Listener, at: V3Like, o: SpatialOptions = {}): Spatial\n```\n\nGain and pan of a sound at `at` for `listener`.\n\n### interface SpatialOptions\n\n- `ref?: number`: Within this distance (m) the sound is at full volume (default 1).\n- `max?: number`: Silent from this distance on (default 30); it fades out over the last fifth.\n- `rolloff?: number`: How fast it falls off past `ref` (default 1: inverse distance, the way sound really does).\n\n### interface Voice\n\nA looping sound steered while it plays (`AudioManager.loop`): a hum, a fire, a waterfall.\n\n- `readonly name: string`\n- `readonly settings: Readonly<Required<VoiceSettings>>`: The values last set (kept headless too, so games and tests can read them).\n- `readonly sounding: boolean`: Really sounding: Web Audio running and the loop started.\n- `readonly stopped: boolean`\n- `set(s: VoiceSettings): void`: Change any of its settings; they glide over a few hundredths of a second (no clicks).\n- `stop(): void`\n\n### interface VoiceSettings\n\n- `volume?: number`: 0..1 (default 1).\n- `pan?: number`: Stereo position -1 (left) .. 1 (right).\n- `muffle?: number`: 0 (clear) .. 1 (behind a thick wall, under water): a low-pass filter closing down.\n- `pitch?: number`: Pitch shift in semitones.\n\n### type VolumeChannel\n\n```ts\ntype VolumeChannel = 'master' | 'sfx' | 'music'\n```\n\n### type Wave\n\n```ts\ntype Wave = 'square' | 'triangle' | 'saw' | 'sine' | 'noise'\n```\n\nRetro sound synthesis as pure functions (no Web Audio): a sound is data (`SoundDef`)\nrendered to samples once, then played as a buffer. Deterministic (seeded noise), so\n| `status(ctx)` | tooling | a one-line summary (`'coins 3/4'`), shown by the check tool |\n| `fingerprint(ctx)` | tooling | the game's own state as a string (positions, counters), part of `engine.fingerprint()` so a replay can be compared |\n| `snapshot(ctx)`, `restore(ctx, state)`, `beforeRestore(ctx)` | save-states | the game's own state as plain data (as it is, not through JSON), and back (`await engine.snapshot()`, `await engine.restore(snap)`); physics bodies are the engine's: keep their handles; `beforeRestore` leaves a mode with its own camera first |\n| `onCameraChange(ctx)`, `eyePosition(ctx)`, `dispose(ctx)` | optional | camera swaps, first-person eye, cleanup |\n| `ready(ctx)` | optional: when the engine starts running the game, and after each `loadGame` | page handles for tools (`window.__MYGAME__`) |\n\n`ctx` (the `GameContext`) is the same object in every hook: `ctx.scene` (three.js scene),\n`ctx.physics`, `ctx.input`, `ctx.camera`, `ctx.audio`, `ctx.particles`, `ctx.hud`,\n`ctx.lights`, `ctx.palette`, `ctx.loadModel`, `ctx.time`, `ctx.random` and `ctx.engine`.\n\n**Physics owns positions; visuals follow.** Bodies move in fixed 60 Hz steps (Rapier).\nMeshes are interpolated between steps, so motion is smooth at any frame rate. A character\ncontroller moves its body in `fixedUpdate`; its model is posed in `update`.\n\n**Scale.** One unit is one metre, Y is up, the ground is usually at y = 0. The hero is 1.6 m\ntall, runs at 6.5 m/s and jumps 1.7 m (double jump 2.4 m, triple 3.5 m; it also grabs ledges).\nAt the default zoom the camera shows about 13.5 m vertically, so one art pixel is about\n5 cm: give shapes at least 0.25 m so they read.\n\n## 3. Quick start\n\nA complete game, and the kit's `game.js`. Walk with WASD or the arrows, jump with Space,\ncollect the four coins.\n\n```js quickstart\n// Coin Garden: walk (WASD / arrows / stick), jump (Space), collect every coin.\nimport { compileClips, ContactShadow, Engine, HERO_CLIPS, HERO_MODEL, HERO_RIG, mergeStaticMeshes, PlatformerCharacter, readMoveInput, setLookLayer, THREE, toonMaterial, withUrlOptions } from './pixel-engine.js';\n\nconst { BoxGeometry, Mesh, Vector3 } = THREE;\n\n/** A box you can see (toon material, palette colour) and stand on (a static collider). */\nfunction block(ctx, [x, y, z], [w, h, d], color) {\n  ctx.physics.addStaticBox({ position: [x, y, z], halfExtents: [w / 2, h / 2, d / 2] });\n  const mesh = new Mesh(new BoxGeometry(w, h, d), toonMaterial(color));\n  mesh.position.set(x, y, z);\n  mesh.castShadow = mesh.receiveShadow = true;\n13:7. Input and touch: `applyDeadzone`, `ButtonEvent`, `DEFAULT_TOUCH_BUTTONS`, `GAMEPAD_BUTTONS`, `GamepadLike`, `Input`, `InputFrame`, `InputState`, `MOUSE_BUTTONS`, `PRESS_WINDOW`, `TOUCH_KEY`, `TouchButton`, `TouchButtonState`, `TouchControls`, `TouchCorner`\n53:Chunky pixels for a resolution: the engine's 320×180 for its 480×270, else two thirds of it.\n66:function computeFraming(viewportWidth: number, viewportHeight: number, devicePixelRatio: number, internal: Resolution, aspect: AspectMode = 'fixed'): Framing\n112:  debugKeys: { resolution: 'F2', mute: null }   // with the panel: R freed for the game, no mute key\n245:function frameView(viewport: Viewport, base: Resolution, aspect: AspectMode, current: Resolution): { framing: Framing; resolution: Resolution }\n367:- `pixelSize?: 'fine' | 'chunky'`: The art resolution: `fine` (the game's, 480×270 by default) or `chunky` (two thirds of it: bigger pixels).\n426:resolution: 'F7' })`), so a shipped game, which has no hotkeys, gets none back.\n485:- `readonly renderer: { readonly mode: RenderMode; readonly baseResolution: Resolution; readonly lookVersion: number; setMode(mode: RenderMode): void; setResolution(resolution: Reso…`\n593:- `aspect: number`: Viewport aspect (width / height), following the art resolution (see `setAspect`).\n596:- `setAspect(aspect: number): void`: Match the art resolution's aspect (adaptive framing changes it with the screen). `update()` calls this from `CameraUpdate.resolution`; the engine also calls it on swap.\n611:- `readonly aspect: number`\n1086:- `get resolution: Resolution`: The actual art resolution; adaptive varies its width, fill varies both dimensions.\n1088:- `setResolution(resolution: Resolution): void`\n1089:- `get aspect: AspectMode`\n1090:- `setAspect(aspect: AspectMode): void`\n2268:### GAMEPAD_BUTTONS\n2271:const GAMEPAD_BUTTONS: Readonly<Record<number, string>>\n2275:KEYMAP). Change `input.gamepadButtons` to remap. Indices follow the W3C \"standard\" layout:\n2294:  (`gamepadButtons`). Polled once per frame by the engine (`beginFrame`).\n2311:- `gamepadButtons: Record<number, string>`: Gamepad button index → key code (defaults: GAMEPAD_BUTTONS).\n2787:- `sync(resolution: Resolution, framing: Framing): void`: Engine: follow the renderer's resolution and framing, and repaint if needed.\n4368:- `get resolution: Resolution`: The actual art resolution; adaptive varies its width, fill varies both dimensions.\n4370:- `setResolution(resolution: Resolution): void`\n4371:- `get aspect: AspectMode`\n4372:- `setAspect(aspect: AspectMode): void`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
