{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_80","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"D='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; sed -n '1,180p' TASK.md; sed -n '1,150p' PLAN.md; sed 's/}/}\\\\n/g' \\\"\"'$D/src/ui/style.css\" | head -100; rg -n '\"'HERO_MODEL|HERO_CLIPS|HERO_RIG|compileClips|withUrlOptions|class Engine|async start\\\\(' \\\"\"'$D/pixel-engine.js\" | head -40'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_80","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"D='A Little Off Balance-Wii Fit-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846'; sed -n '1,180p' TASK.md; sed -n '1,150p' PLAN.md; sed 's/}/}\\\\n/g' \\\"\"'$D/src/ui/style.css\" | head -100; rg -n '\"'HERO_MODEL|HERO_CLIPS|HERO_RIG|compileClips|withUrlOptions|class Engine|async start\\\\(' \\\"\"'$D/pixel-engine.js\" | head -40'","aggregated_output":"SOURCE: Wii Fit\n\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\n\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\n\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\n\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\n\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\n\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/riftlight/releases/latest/download/pixel-engine.zip, the riftlight engine kit. Keep the engine (pixel-engine.js) as it ships, as its guide says, and add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\n\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\n\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\n\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\n\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\n\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-gpt-6.1-terra-max-20261104-221846].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\n\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\n\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\nEffort: Spend effort however you see fit.\n\n## This computer\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\n\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\n\n### What this computer doesn't have\n\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\n\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n\n### Sharing this computer\n\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\n\n## Goal\n\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\n\nScope\n\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\n\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\n\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\n\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\n\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\n\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\n\n## Done\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\n\n## Source Information\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:18:46\n- Source: the work it's based on\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n- Art Direction: style used\n- Animation: system used\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n- Online: network features used, or none\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\n- Research: research done, or none\n- Environment: Codex CLI (headless); Linux\n- Model: OpenAI, gpt-6.1-terra, reasoning effort max\n- Usage: write \"added on import\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\n\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\": \"\", \"source\": \"\", \"plan\": \"\", \"genre\": \"\", \"perspective\": \"\", \"tech\": \"\", \"artDirection\": \"\", \"animation\": \"\", \"audio\": \"\", \"online\": \"\", \"testing\": \"\", \"research\": \"\", \"environment\": \"\", \"model\": \"\", \"usage\": \"added on import\"},\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\"],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n    \"tagline\": \"a few words to go under the title\",\n    \"description\": \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n    \"type\": \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n    \"genre\": \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\",\n    \"source\": \"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\",\n    \"series\": \"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\",\n    \"players\": \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"the controls, in one line\",\n    \"tags\": [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\"]\n  }\n}\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\n\nTesting Feedback\n\n- How many issues did testing catch?\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\nEngine Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about the engine?\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\nPrompt Feedback\n\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\n- What was confusing?\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\n- What would you change in this prompt?\n# Game plan: an original fitness comedy inspired by Wii Fit\n\nYou are the builder. Build this game from this plan and your brief. Nobody will answer questions, so where the plan leaves something open, decide and move on.\n\n- **The title is yours.** Pick it the way your brief says. It lives in one constant (`src/data/title.js`) and nothing else hard-codes it. In this plan `{TITLE}` stands for it.\n- **Paths are relative to your dev folder.** \"Root\" means its parent, where your brief puts the deliverable html and the exit-interview file. Use the names your brief gives.\n- **✔ means the director ran it** on this machine against engine kit 0.46.0 and hub client 1.4.0, in the form written here. Don't re-derive it. If your download is newer, read its changelog from 0.46.0 on.\n- **Read in this order:** this plan; the kit's `GUIDE.md` and `ASSETS.md`, whole; the hub's `README.md`, whole. Search `API.md` and `LIBRARY.md`; don't read them whole.\n- Your brief's rules on folders, ports, processes, browsers and test reporting still apply. They are repeated here only where they shape the work.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**A week at a spotless wellness island run by a small, polite, deeply judgmental bathroom scale. Your whole body is one dot. Keep it in the circle.**\n\n- **Genre:** fitness and balance minigame collection, inside a light life-sim week. (Catalogue genre: Sports.)\n- **Perspective:** isometric on the island; fixed front-on for class activities; other fixed views per activity (section 5).\n- **Length:** a story week of 7 short days, 16 minutes at the quickest and about an hour for a player who tries everything. Everything is also playable at once from Drop-In Class.\n\n### Pillars (use these to settle any open question)\n\n1. **You are a dot.** Almost everything is one verb: lean. The left stick, WASD or the mouse moves your centre of balance, and it has weight and sway. Poses, reps, hoops, slacklines and the finale are all different ways of moving or holding the Dot.\n2. **Politely judged.** Everything you do gets a calm, clinical, faintly wounding remark. You cannot lose. You can only be noted.\n3. **One small day at a time.** Short sessions, a morning number, a chart, a jar slowly filling.\n4. **Always in class.** Other people are always beside you: classmates alone, family on the couch, friends as ghosts online. Everyone wobbles.\n\n### What it takes from the source, and how it stays original\n\n| Distinctive in the source | Here |\n|---|---|\n| Your body is the controller; a centre-of-balance dot | The Dot: real sway physics on a stick, keys or mouse |\n| A talking device that politely judges you | Ounce, a mint retro dial scale whose dial is its face |\n| A calm instructor demonstrating | Coach Plumb, 81, unblinking, never off vertical |\n| Daily check-in, one number that sums you up, a graph | Weigh-In, Wobble Age, a line chart with a twist |\n| Yoga, strength, aerobics, balance games | Four stations on the island |\n| Minutes banked toward unlocks | Drips in the Sweat Jar |\n| A bright, clean, soft-spoken world | White tile plaza on a pastel island made of giant gym equipment |\n| A household sharing one device | Family Hour on one TV, AI Hub sessions online |\n\n**Do not use** any Nintendo name, character, look, music, level or minigame name: no Wii, Mii, Balance Board, Wii Fit Trainer, Wuhu Island or Fit Age. No white-board host, no sound-alike tunes, no channel-style menus. Mechanics may rhyme; everything you can see, hear or read is new.\n\n**Taste rule:** no jokes about a real person's weight, body shape or eating, and no health claims. Ounce judges wobble, knees, posture and life choices, never bodies. The game asks for no real health data. The About screen says: \"This is not medical advice. Ounce is a scale.\"\n\n---\n\n## 2. World and cast\n\n**Tare Island** (\"Everything here starts at zero.\") is a tiny resort whose landscape is exercise equipment at enormous scale: Kettlebell Volcano (dormant, mostly), Foam Roller Hills, the Dumbbell Bridge, medicine-ball boulders. A white-tiled plaza sits at its heart, ringed by lawn, sand and calm sea.\n\n| Who | What | Voice |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Ounce** | The host, a scale. Hops after you on the island. Eyes and a red needle on a round dial: the needle is its eyebrow, and it whips, droops or spins with mood. | High, clipped, polite. \"I'd like to weigh in on that.\" |\n| **Coach Plumb** | 81, silver bun, cyan tracksuit, a plumb bob hanging from one wrist. Demonstrates everything without effort. | Low, slow, deadpan. |\n| **Marguerite** | 74, classmate. Has had everything replaced and tops every leaderboard. Friendly menace. | Warm, unhurried. |\n| **Dennis** | 47, classmate. Here after \"an incident with a deck chair\". Overconfident, always nearly injured. Beatable by Day 3. | Quick, hearty. |\n| **June** | 12, Marguerite's granddaughter. Excellent and bored. | Flat. |\n| **Kevin** | A seagull. Lands on people. Ounce speaks of him as staff. | Squawks. |\n\n**The story in one breath:** you arrive by ferry; Ounce weighs you and gives you an alarming Wobble Age. Through the week the classmates hint that something is off (\"Has anyone ever checked that scale?\"; Coach hangs her plumb line beside Ounce: \"You are leaning.\"; Kevin sits on Ounce and the dial reads 7). On Day 7 you face **The Long Stand**: 90 seconds of standing still while the whole island tries to distract you. Then Ounce confesses: *\"I have been adding seven. To everyone. For years. It felt motivating.\"* The chart of your week visibly slides down seven years. Credits. *\"See you tomorrow. Bring your knees.\"* Play continues as an open-ended Week 2.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Structure of play\n\n### Menu map\n\n```\nTitle (\"Press any button. Gently.\")      idle 10 s on title or any menu -> Attract\n└ Main Menu\n  ├ The Week        pick one of 3 guest slots -> Continue day N / start a week -> Island\n  ├ Drop-In Class   any activity at once, all open, records only, no Drips or story\n  ├ Family Hour     1-4 players on this screen -> Join -> The Medley -> Podium\n  ├ Settings\n  └ About & Credits\n```\n\nIn an AI Hub session none of this appears: the game opens on its own card, then the Medley (section 12).\n\n### Starting a week\n\nName entry, one of 6 preset avatars (the Locker Room changes it later), then the ferry cutscene and Ounce's introduction, which teaches the Dot before the first test: \"This dot is you. Keep it in the circle.\" Then the first Weigh-In. Ounce also asks \"How old do you feel? This is not binding.\" with a number picker; the answer is used once, in the joke when the Wobble Age arrives, and never saved.\n\n### A day in The Week\n\n1. **Morning Weigh-In** (about 45 s, section 6): balance test, one absurd question, today's Wobble Age.\n2. **Free time on the island:** walk between stations, do what you like. Coach's prescription (a daily goal) pays a bonus. Ounce follows and comments.\n3. **Hammock** ends the day once you've done at least one activity. Leaving early earns a guilt-trip, never a block.\n4. **Evening Report** (about 20 s): minutes, Drips, stars, the chart, tomorrow's \"concern\", new unlocks. The light goes to dusk (`applySky(engine, skyAt(19))`), then fade to morning.\n\n### The week\n\n| Day | Test | Coach's prescription (+5 Drips) | Opens today | Story beat |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Stillness | Holding Still and one other | Apologetic Tree, Startled Heron, Squats of Regret, Hoop Economics | Ferry. \"Please step on me.\" \"Ow.\" \"This dot is you. Keep it in the circle. That's it. That's the game. Everything else is decoration.\" |\n| 2 | Lean | 2★ in anything | Coconut Header, Apology Lunges, Reluctant Warrior (adds breathing), Locker Room | Dennis explains the deck chair. Nobody asked. |\n| 3 | Chase | Something you haven't done | Step Class, Slackline, The Chair Nobody Asked For | Marguerite: \"Has anyone ever checked that scale?\" Ounce: \"I'd like to weigh in on that. No.\" |\n| 4 | Stillness | 3 activities | Vitamin Tray, Overhead Mortgage, Tax Season | Coach hangs her plumb line by Ounce. \"You are leaning.\" \"The island is leaning.\" |\n| 5 | Lean | Beat Dennis at anything | Island Jog, Pier Jump, Plank Negotiation | Kevin sits on Ounce. The dial reads 7. Nobody says anything. |\n| 6 | Chase | 3★ anywhere | Hard versions of everything | Ounce rehearses a speech, badly, behind the Sweat Jar. |\n| 7 | Stillness | Stand. | The Long Stand, then the ending | The confession. The chart slides. Credits. |\n\n### Progress\n\n- **Stars:** every activity gives 0-3★ from thresholds in its data.\n- **Drips:** `2 + 3 × stars + floor(playSeconds / 30)`, plus the +5 goal bonus. They fill the **Sweat Jar**.\n- **Jar milestones** at 10, 25, 45, 70, 100, 135 and 175 Drips each unlock a Locker Room item (second headband, towel cape, \"Confidence: no visible effect\", and so on) and a remark.\n- **Records:** per activity, compared with data-table scores for Dennis (low), June (mid-high) and Marguerite (high). A classmate you beat reacts in the Report.\n- **Saves:** `localStorage`, versioned JSON, one key, 3 guest slots, written after every activity and day. Handle missing storage (Node, private mode) with an in-memory fallback.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The core: the Dot\n\nEverything hangs on this feeling good, so it comes first. A stick mapped straight to position would be trivial, because you would just hold still. So the Dot is an unstable weight: you apply force, and it drifts, sways and overshoots.\n\n### The model ✔ (simulated with human-like bots; these are the defaults)\n\n```js\n// src/core/dot.js: pure, no engine imports. Step in fixedUpdate at 60 Hz. p is in -1.2..1.2.\nexport class Dot {\n  x = 0; y = 0; vx = 0; vy = 0; ux = 0; uy = 0; t = 0;\n  constructor(rand) { this.ph = [rand(), rand(), rand(), rand()].map((r) => r * 6.283); }\n  step(dt, inX, inY, T, m = {}) { // T: tunables. m: per-activity modifiers.\n    this.t += dt;\n    const k = Math.min(1, dt / T.dotLag);\n    this.ux += (inX - this.ux) * k; this.uy += (inY - this.uy) * k;\n    const n = T.dotNoise * (m.noise ?? 1), inst = T.dotInstability * (m.inst ?? 1), auth = T.dotAuthority * (m.auth ?? 1);\n    const nx = n * (Math.sin(1.7 * this.t + this.ph[0]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(3.1 * this.t + this.ph[1]));\n    const ny = n * (Math.sin(1.3 * this.t + this.ph[2]) + 0.6 * Math.sin(2.7 * this.t + this.ph[3]));\n    this.vx += (inst * this.x + auth * this.ux - T.dotDamping * this.vx + nx + (m.bx ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.vy += (inst * this.y + auth * this.uy - T.dotDamping * this.vy + ny + (m.by ?? 0)) * dt;\n    this.x += this.vx * dt; this.y += this.vy * dt;\n    const d = Math.hypot(this.x, this.y);\n    if (d > 1.2) { const s = 1.2 / d; this.x *= s; this.y *= s; this.vx *= 0.3; this.vy *= 0.3; }\n  }\n}\n// Defaults: dotInstability 0.6, dotAuthority 3.0, dotDamping 3.4, dotNoise 0.8, dotLag 0.08\n```\n\nTop speed is about 0.9 units/s, so a lean feels like shifting a heavy marble. Impulses (a sneeze, a gust) add directly to `vx`/`vy`.\n\n### Lean input\n\nAll devices produce one vector `u` in the unit disc, read per lane through a **driver** (section 16).\n\n| Device | `u` |\n|---|---|\n| Stick | `ctx.input.moveAxis()`; set `input.gamepadDeadzone = 0.12` |\n| Keyboard | WASD / arrows, smoothed (attack 0.10 s, release 0.06 s). **Hold Shift for half lean.** |\n@import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Fredoka:wght@400;500;600;700&display=swap');\n:root{font-family:'DM Sans',system-ui,sans-serif;color:#253e43;background:#cce8dc;font-synthesis:none;--ink:#253e43;--cream:#fbfaf0;--mint:#dcefe4;--orange:#ef795f;--yellow:#f7c96f;--line:#d3e0d8}\n*{box-sizing:border-box}\nhtml,body{width:100%;height:100%;margin:0;overflow:hidden}\nbutton,input,select{font:inherit}\nbutton{cursor:pointer}\nbutton:focus-visible,input:focus-visible,select:focus-visible{outline:4px solid #f19d5d;outline-offset:3px}\n#world,#ui{position:fixed;inset:0}\n#world{background:linear-gradient(155deg,#deefe4 0%,#c5e6dc 54%,#8dcfca 100%);overflow:hidden}\n#world canvas{display:block;width:100%;height:100%}\n#ui{z-index:2;overflow:hidden;pointer-events:none}\n.screen{position:absolute;inset:0;display:flex;align-items:center;padding:clamp(20px,4vw,64px);pointer-events:none}\n.screen>*{pointer-events:auto}\n.panel{width:min(550px,100%);max-height:100%;overflow:auto;scrollbar-width:thin;scrollbar-color:#a8bfb2 transparent}\n.glass{background:rgba(251,250,240,.96);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.8);border-radius:28px;box-shadow:0 18px 60px #285a4c1c;padding:clamp(22px,3vw,40px)}\n.brand{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:9px;font:700 13px 'Fredoka',sans-serif;letter-spacing:.13em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#558477}\n.brand-icon{display:grid;place-items:center;width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:10px;background:var(--ink);color:#fff5dd;font-size:16px}\n.eyebrow{font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#73988a}\n.title{font:600 clamp(54px,7.5vw,100px)/.91 'Fredoka',system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.055em;margin:30px 0 16px;color:#294943}\n.title span{color:#e97960}\n.lede{font-size:clamp(15px,1.5vw,18px);line-height:1.5;color:#607b70;max-width:390px}\n.title-tag{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:8px 12px;border-radius:999px;background:#f7dca2;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.12em;color:#785c29;text-transform:uppercase}\n.title-tag:before{content:'';width:7px;height:7px;background:#e98d5e;border-radius:100%}\n.menu{display:grid;gap:9px;margin-top:27px}\n.button{min-height:54px;border:0;border-radius:15px;padding:13px 18px;display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px;text-align:left;font-weight:700;color:var(--ink);background:#e8f1e8;transition:transform .15s,background .15s,box-shadow .15s}\n.button:hover,.button.focused{transform:translateX(4px);background:#dcece0}\n.button.primary{color:#fff;background:#ef795f;box-shadow:0 5px 0 #d86350}\n.button.primary:hover,.button.primary.focused{background:#e76a52}\n.button .arrow{margin-left:auto;font-size:19px;font-weight:400}\n.button.small{min-height:42px;padding:9px 14px;font-size:13px;border-radius:11px}\n.button.ghost{background:#f2f4e9}\n.menu-row{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:9px}\n.footnote{margin-top:23px;display:flex;gap:12px;align-items:center;color:#779288;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.05em}\n.key{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;min-width:24px;height:24px;padding:0 6px;border:1px solid #bed0c4;border-radius:7px;background:#fff;font-size:11px;color:#49685c}\n.hero-note{position:absolute;right:6vw;bottom:6vh;text-align:right;color:#fff;text-shadow:0 1px 10px #28574e65;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.16em;text-transform:uppercase;line-height:1.7}\n.hero-note strong{font:500 29px/1.2 'Fredoka',sans-serif;letter-spacing:0;display:block;text-transform:none}\n.topline{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:12px;margin-bottom:22px}\n.back{border:0;background:#e9f1e8;color:#456b5c;border-radius:50%;width:40px;height:40px;font-size:19px}\n.heading{font:600 clamp(31px,4vw,45px)/1 'Fredoka',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.035em;margin:6px 0 10px}\n.description{margin:0;color:#6b8378;line-height:1.5;font-size:14px}\n.stack{display:grid;gap:9px;margin-top:22px}\n.list-item{position:relative;border:1px solid #e1e9de;border-radius:16px;background:#fff;min-height:66px;padding:12px 14px;display:flex;align-items:center;text-align:left;gap:12px;width:100%;color:var(--ink)}\n.list-item:hover,.list-item.focused{border-color:#ef9f88;box-shadow:0 3px 0 #f5c3b0}\n.list-item:disabled{opacity:.52;cursor:not-allowed}\n.list-item .icon{flex:none;width:41px;height:41px;border-radius:13px;display:grid;place-items:center;background:#dcefe5;font-size:21px}\n.list-item .name{display:block;font:600 16px 'Fredoka',sans-serif}\n.list-item .sub{display:block;color:#89a094;font-size:11px;margin-top:3px}\n.list-item .arrow{margin-left:auto;color:#98afa1}\n.badge{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;padding:6px 10px;border-radius:999px;font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;background:#e5f0e6;color:#658679}\n.badge.sun{background:#ffefd2;color:#a2752c}\n.badge.coral{background:#ffebe4;color:#c96951}\n.scroll-list{max-height:min(45vh,400px);overflow:auto;padding:1px 4px 4px 1px}\n.profile-card{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:12px}\n.profile-avatar{height:44px;width:44px;border-radius:50%;background:#f3c394;display:grid;place-items:center;font-size:23px}\n.profile-info strong{font:600 15px 'Fredoka',sans-serif}\n.profile-info small{display:block;color:#86a094;margin-top:3px}\n.island-screen{align-items:stretch}\n.island-screen .panel{width:min(520px,100%)}\n.stats{display:flex;gap:7px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:16px 0}\n.stats span{padding:7px 10px;border-radius:9px;background:#edf3e9;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:#6d8b7d}\n.actions{display:flex;gap:9px;margin-top:18px}\n.actions>*{flex:1}\n.section-label{margin:21px 0 10px;color:#8aa296;font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700}\n.guide{display:flex;gap:13px;align-items:flex-start;background:#e5f1e7;padding:14px;border-radius:15px;margin-top:21px}\n.ounce{flex:none;width:45px;height:40px;border-radius:13px 13px 16px 16px;background:#fdfcf3;box-shadow:inset 0 -5px 0 #dce3d9;position:relative}\n.ounce:before{content:'• ᴗ •';position:absolute;left:6px;top:11px;white-space:nowrap;color:#6e8f80;font-size:12px}\n.guide p{margin:0;color:#618071;font-size:13px;line-height:1.4}\n.game-screen{display:block;padding:0}\n.game-hud{position:absolute;inset:0;padding:clamp(16px,3vw,38px);display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:space-between;pointer-events:none}\n.game-top{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px}\n.game-title{background:#fbfaf0ed;border-radius:18px;padding:14px 18px;box-shadow:0 8px 26px #214f3f1a;max-width:min(410px,60vw)}\n.game-title strong{display:block;font:600 clamp(18px,2.6vw,27px) 'Fredoka',sans-serif}\n.game-title small{font-size:10px;letter-spacing:.11em;color:#8ba195;font-weight:700}\n.game-time{background:#fbfaf0ed;border-radius:18px;padding:12px 18px;text-align:center;font:600 30px 'Fredoka',sans-serif}\n.game-time small{display:block;font:700 9px 'DM Sans',sans-serif;letter-spacing:.15em;color:#8ba195}\n.game-bottom{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:flex-end;gap:15px}\n.balance-card{background:#fbfaf0f5;border-radius:24px;padding:15px 20px;box-shadow:0 8px 30px #214f3f20;pointer-events:auto}\n.balance-inner{display:flex;align-items:center;gap:18px}\n.dial{width:clamp(125px,18vw,210px);aspect-ratio:1;position:relative;border-radius:50%;background:radial-gradient(circle,#f9fbf2 0 28%,#dceee2 28.5% 29.5%,#edf5e9 30% 58%,#c8e0d2 58.5% 59.5%,#f5f8ee 60%);border:2px solid #d5e6d9;touch-action:none}\n.dial:before,.dial:after{content:'';position:absolute;background:#bdd7c8;opacity:.65}\n.dial:before{height:1px;left:0;right:0;top:50%}\n.dial:after{width:1px;top:0;bottom:0;left:50%}\n.dot{position:absolute;left:50%;top:50%;width:18px;height:18px;margin:-9px;border-radius:50%;background:#f2765c;border:3px solid white;box-shadow:0 2px 5px #d47e6770;z-index:1}\n.balance-text{width:125px}\n.balance-text strong{font:600 19px/1.1 'Fredoka',sans-serif}\n.balance-text p{font-size:11px;line-height:1.5;color:#789184}\n.meter{height:8px;border-radius:8px;background:#dbe8dd;overflow:hidden;margin-top:9px}\n.meter span{height:100%;display:block;background:#ed856a;border-radius:8px;transition:width .1s}\n.game-tip{background:#fbfaf0ed;border-radius:15px;padding:12px 15px;color:#729084;font-weight:700;font-size:11px;line-height:1.6;text-align:right}\n.game-tip .key{margin:0 2px}\n.score-hero{font:600 clamp(60px,9vw,94px)/1 'Fredoka',sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.04em;color:#e77c61;margin:18px 0 0}\n.stars{color:#f3bb53;font-size:31px;letter-spacing:4px}\n.result-box{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;border-top:1px solid #e3e9df;margin-top:18px;padding-top:17px;font-size:13px;color:#718b7e}\n.field{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:14px;padding:13px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #e8eee5}\n.field label{font-weight:700;font-size:13px}\n.field input[type=range]{width:46%;accent-color:#ee8165}\n.field select{border:1px solid #d7e5d8;background:white;border-radius:9px;padding:7px}\n.field input[type=text]{width:62%;border:1px solid #d7e5d8;background:white;border-radius:9px;padding:10px;color:#34564a}\n.about-text{font-size:13px;line-height:1.6;color:#607a6d}\n.about-text h3{color:#355a4a;font:600 